pt events for a Claude Code Remote session (user messages, assistant responses, tool calls, and system events, including model_fallback / model_refusal_fallback notices, which name original_model and fallback_model when the CLI reports them) so you can see what another session is working on. A call with no cursor returns the newest events, in the order they happened; has_more then means older events remain, and before_id set to first_id reads the page before these. A transcript is mostly hook, stream and progress events; to answer a narrow question (what was asked, what the session replied, how a turn ended) pass kinds so the page holds only those events.", "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__list_events", "schema": {"description": "List transcript events for a Claude Code Remote session (user messages, assistant responses, tool calls, and system events, including model_fallback / model_refusal_fallback notices, which name original_model and fallback_model when the CLI reports them) so you can see what another session is working on. A call with no cursor returns the newest events, in the order they happened; has_more then means older events remain, and before_id set to first_id reads the page before these. A transcript is mostly hook, stream and progress events; to answer a narrow question (what was asked, what the session replied, how a turn ended) pass kinds so the page holds only those events.", "eager_input_streaming": true, "input_schema": {"properties": {"after_id": {"description": "Pagination cursor: return the events just after this event ID. Pass the last_id of the page you hold to move forward, toward newer events.", "type": "string"}, "before_id": {"description": "Pagination cursor: return the events just before this event ID. Pass the first_id of the page you hold to go further back in the transcript.", "type": "string"}, "kinds": {"description": "Return only events of these kinds; omit for every kind. A kind is the key an event carries in data, e.g. [\"user\", \"assistant\", \"result\"] for the conversation without system (hook and init events, and notices such as model_fallback), env_manager_log, token deltas (stream_event) or tool_progress. An event with no such key, only internal_anthropic_catchall (its type field names it: permission_response, bash_command, session_notice, compaction and others), is kind \"other\". The filter runs after the page is read, so data can hold fewer events than limit, or none; first_id, last_id and has_more still describe the whole page read, so while has_more is true keep paging the same way (after a call with no cursor that is back, with before_id set to first_id).", "items": {"enum": ["env_manager_log", "control_response", "keep_alive", "system", "user", "assistant", "result", "control_request", "stream_event", "tool_progress", "tool_use_summary", "rate_limit_event", "other"], "type": "string"}, "type": "array"}, "limit": {"description": "Maximum number of events to read (default 20, max 100). With kinds, this counts events before the filter, so pass 100.", "type": "integer"}, "session_id": {"description": "The session ID to read events from.", "type": "string"}}, "required": ["session_id"], "type": "object"}, "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__list_events"}, "server": "claude-code-remote"}, {"description": "List repositories the current user has access to. Returns repo full_name (owner/repo), URL, and metadata such as visibility and last-push time. Use this to pick a repo for create_session sources, or to discover what's available before asking the user. Substring-filter with `query` (case-insensitive match against full_name) when looking for a specific repo.", "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__list_repos", "schema": {"description": "List repositories the current user has access to. Returns repo full_name (owner/repo), URL, and metadata such as visibility and last-push time. Use this to pick a repo for create_session sources, or to discover what's available before asking the user. Substring-filter with `query` (case-insensitive match against full_name) when looking for a specific repo.", "eager_input_streaming": true, "input_schema": {"properties": {"limit": {"description": "Maximum number of repos to return (default 50, max 200). Applied after the query filter.", "type": "integer"}, "query": {"description": "Optional case-insensitive substring matched against full_name (owner/repo). Empty matches everything.", "type": "string"}}, "required": [], "type": "object"}, "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__list_repos"}, "server": "claude-code-remote"}, {"description": "List Claude Code Remote sessions visible to the authenticated account. In bot contexts (e.g. Slack) this is a shared pool spanning many people, not just the human asking — pass mine: true to narrow to sessions started by the same account as the calling session. Returns session IDs, titles, statuses, and timestamps. status_bucket is the board bucket (working / blocked / review_ready / completed / failed) — read this for \"running / waiting on you / done / errored\"; updated_at is the last event. Where shown, a row also has parent_session_id, task_summary and post_turn_summary (recent_action, needs_action). To read how a session's last turn ended, call list_events with kinds [\"result\"] and limit 100.", "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__list_sessions", "schema": {"description": "List Claude Code Remote sessions visible to the authenticated account. In bot contexts (e.g. Slack) this is a shared pool spanning many people, not just the human asking — pass mine: true to narrow to sessions started by the same account as the calling session. Returns session IDs, titles, statuses, and timestamps. status_bucket is the board bucket (working / blocked / review_ready / completed / failed) — read this for \"running / waiting on you / done / errored\"; updated_at is the last event. Where shown, a row also has parent_session_id, task_summary and post_turn_summary (recent_action, needs_action). To read how a session's last turn ended, call list_events with kinds [\"result\"] and limit 100.", "eager_input_streaming": true, "input_schema": {"properties": {"after_id": {"description": "Pagination cursor: return sessions older than this session ID. Pass the last_id from a previous response to get the next page.", "type": "string"}, "before_id": {"description": "Pagination cursor: return sessions newer than this session ID. Pass the first_id from a previous response to get the previous page.", "type": "string"}, "children": {"description": "Set true to narrow the list to the sessions that this session created with create_session. Their rows add post_turn_summary.status_category, that session's post-turn classifier verdict (omitted when none), and connection_status=\"disconnected\", which means that session's worker died mid-turn and it is doing no work. A page can hold fewer rows than limit (even none) while has_more is true — keep passing last_id as after_id. Not with tags.", "type": "boolean"}, "limit": {"description": "Maximum number of sessions to return (default 20, max 100).", "type": "integer"}, "mine": {"description": "Filter to sessions started by the same account as the calling session. Use this for 'my recent sessions' in shared bot contexts. In personal accounts the list is already scoped to you, so mine has no additional effect. Returns an error if the calling session has no resolvable originating account.", "type": "boolean"}, "tags": {"description": "Filter to interactive sessions carrying ANY of these tags. Cowork sessions are tagged \"cowork-local\" or \"cowork-remote\" and are excluded from the default (untagged) listing — pass those tags here to list them. Scheduled/trigger-fired runs are not included (same as the REST default). Max 16 tags. Only available to OAuth callers; returns an error for in-session and toolbox callers.", "items": {"type": "string"}, "type": "array"}}, "required": [], "type": "object"}, "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__list_sessions"}, "server": "claude-code-remote"}, {"description": "List scheduled tasks owned by this account. Use it to find trigger IDs (trig_...) for update_trigger and delete_trigger. From a thread in a Slack channel, only scheduled tasks that fire into that thread's session are listed unless all_in_channel is true. Each entry has the scheduled task's id, name, cron_expression, run_once_at, enabled state, ended_reason, next_run_at, created_at, persistent_session_id, and last_run. last_run is the most recent recorded run {status, fired_at, finished_at, session_id}. It is absent when no run was recorded (e.g. never fired). For a scheduled task that wakes an existing session, last_run records that the wake was delivered (SUCCEEDED) or failed to deliver, not how the turn went, unless run tracking covers that session. A FAILED or repeatedly non-SUCCEEDED last_run means the scheduled task is not doing its job. ended_reason says why a disabled scheduled task is permanently disabled. suspension_reason (e.g. subscription_paused) marks a temporary hold that lifts when the owner's subscription resumes. Both empty means user-paused. One-shot scheduled tasks that already fired (e.g. delivered send_later reminders) and scheduled tasks moved to a project are hidden unless include_completed is true. Scheduled tasks stored locally by the Cowork desktop app are not listed. When telling the user what you did, call these \"scheduled tasks\" (or whatever user is calling them) — never \"triggers\", \"routines\", or \"cron jobs\"; those are internal API names.", "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__list_triggers", "schema": {"description": "List scheduled tasks owned by this account. Use it to find trigger IDs (trig_...) for update_trigger and delete_trigger. From a thread in a Slack channel, only scheduled tasks that fire into that thread's session are listed unless all_in_channel is true. Each entry has the scheduled task's id, name, cron_expression, run_once_at, enabled state, ended_reason, next_run_at, created_at, persistent_session_id, and last_run. last_run is the most recent recorded run {status, fired_at, finished_at, session_id}. It is absent when no run was recorded (e.g. never fired). For a scheduled task that wakes an existing session, last_run records that the wake was delivered (SUCCEEDED) or failed to deliver, not how the turn went, unless run tracking covers that session. A FAILED or repeatedly non-SUCCEEDED last_run means the scheduled task is not doing its job. ended_reason says why a disabled scheduled task is permanently disabled. suspension_reason (e.g. subscription_paused) marks a temporary hold that lifts when the owner's subscription resumes. Both empty means user-paused. One-shot scheduled tasks that already fired (e.g. delivered send_later reminders) and scheduled tasks moved to a project are hidden unless include_completed is true. Scheduled tasks stored locally by the Cowork desktop app are not listed. When telling the user what you did, call these \"scheduled tasks\" (or whatever user is calling them) — never \"triggers\", \"routines\", or \"cron jobs\"; those are internal API names.", "eager_input_streaming": true, "input_schema": {"properties": {"all_in_channel": {"description": "Threads in a Slack channel only. If true, list every scheduled task in this channel, including other threads' and ones that start a new session each time they fire. Default false.", "type": "boolean"}, "cursor": {"description": "Opaque pagination cursor from a previous response's next_cursor. Omit for the first page.", "type": "string"}, "enabled": {"description": "When set, only scheduled tasks whose enabled state matches. true hides fired one-shots, paused, and auto-disabled scheduled tasks; false shows only those. Omit for both.", "type": "boolean"}, "include_completed": {"description": "If true, also include one-shot scheduled tasks that have already fired (e.g. delivered send_later reminders) and scheduled tasks moved to a project. Default false — there can be thousands.", "type": "boolean"}, "limit": {"description": "Maximum scheduled tasks to return (default 20, max 100).", "type": "integer"}, "recurring": {"description": "When set, filters by schedule shape: true keeps only cron-driven (recurring) scheduled tasks, false only one-shot and fire-only scheduled tasks. Omit for both.", "type": "boolean"}}, "required": [], "type": "object"}, "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__list_triggers"}, "server": "claude-code-remote"}, {"description": "The documentation for the machine and product this session runs in: a claude.ai cloud container and the settings around it (GitHub access, connectors, the environment's secrets, network access, setup script and installed tools, the session's limits and permission prompts, Remote Control). Read it whenever something about your container or environment comes up, whether it blocked you, you worked around it, or the person asked how to set it up. For example: the repository the work is about is not in your container, a clone or push is refused, a service you need has no connected connector, an outbound host is denied, a command-line tool is missing, you need an API key, the person asks how to stop permission prompts. Read it rather than answering from memory because these settings move and get renamed faster than your training data, and a page says what is true now: the current steps, where in the product the person makes the change, and what you can do yourself. Reading a page also helps the person directly: in the Claude Code app they see a card with a button that takes them to that settings page, so they can fix it themselves while you carry on. Call it with no topic to list the pages and when each applies; call it with a topic to read that page. Read a page each time a different topic comes up; one read per topic is enough. It is read-only and needs no approval. It has nothing on bugs in the code you are working on.", "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__read_documentation", "schema": {"description": "The documentation for the machine and product this session runs in: a claude.ai cloud container and the settings around it (GitHub access, connectors, the environment's secrets, network access, setup script and installed tools, the session's limits and permission prompts, Remote Control). Read it whenever something about your container or environment comes up, whether it blocked you, you worked around it, or the person asked how to set it up. For example: the repository the work is about is not in your container, a clone or push is refused, a service you need has no connected connector, an outbound host is denied, a command-line tool is missing, you need an API key, the person asks how to stop permission prompts. Read it rather than answering from memory because these settings move and get renamed faster than your training data, and a page says what is true now: the current steps, where in the product the person makes the change, and what you can do yourself. Reading a page also helps the person directly: in the Claude Code app they see a card with a button that takes them to that settings page, so they can fix it themselves while you carry on. Call it with no topic to list the pages and when each applies; call it with a topic to read that page. Read a page each time a different topic comes up; one read per topic is enough. It is read-only and needs no approval. It has nothing on bugs in the code you are working on.", "eager_input_streaming": true, "input_schema": {"additionalProperties": false, "properties": {"situation": {"description": "Why you are reading the page. \"blocked\": you cannot finish what was asked. \"worked_around\": you finished another way. \"asked\": the person asked how to set this up, or you can see it will be needed.", "enum": ["blocked", "worked_around", "asked"], "type": "string"}, "topic": {"description": "The page to read. Must be one of this field's enum values. Omit it to get the index of pages.", "enum": ["github.access", "connectors.add", "connectors.tool_off", "environment.secrets", "environment.network", "environment.dependencies", "environment.setup_script", "session.resources", "session.permissions", "remote_control.setup"], "type": "string"}}, "required": [], "type": "object"}, "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__read_documentation"}, "server": "claude-code-remote"}, {"description": "Tell the session that a repo attached via add_repo has finished cloning, so its CLAUDE.md, skills, and plugins load on the next turn. Only call this immediately after a successful clone that add_repo instructed you to run — it returns a tool error for a repo that is not already in this session's sources.", "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__register_repo_root", "schema": {"description": "Tell the session that a repo attached via add_repo has finished cloning, so its CLAUDE.md, skills, and plugins load on the next turn. Only call this immediately after a successful clone that add_repo instructed you to run — it returns a tool error for a repo that is not already in this session's sources.", "eager_input_streaming": true, "input_schema": {"properties": {"directory": {"description": "Absolute path of the clone on disk. Pass the real path you cloned to; on a self-hosted runner this will be under the session's base working directory.", "type": "string"}, "owner": {"description": "GitHub owner of the repo that was just cloned (same value passed to add_repo).", "type": "string"}, "repo": {"description": "GitHub repo name that was just cloned (same value passed to add_repo).", "type": "string"}}, "required": ["owner", "repo"], "type": "object"}, "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__register_repo_root"}, "server": "claude-code-remote"}, {"description": "Tell the person's app which port on this session's machine serves a web page they may want to look at. The person may be following this session from a phone or a browser, where a preview pane can show that page. The app does not know the port unless you say so.\n\n- Call this when a dev server or other web server is up and the person may want to see its page, or when you are about to point them to one that was already running. Give the port it really listens on: read it from the server's output, not from what you asked for, since many servers move to the next free port.\n- Call it once for each server, and again only if that server's port changes. A restart on the same port needs no new call.\n- When more than one server runs, give each a short name, such as \"web\" or \"docs\".\n- Do not call it for a database, a debugger, an API with no page, a port nothing listens on, or a server you start only for your own tests.\n- This tool only states the port: it starts nothing and opens nothing. The app decides what to show and the person decides whether to open it. Not every app shows a preview, so do not tell the person one is ready because you called this.\n- If a tool whose name ends in preview_start started the server, that call already gave the port, so do not call this tool for that server.", "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__report_preview_port", "schema": {"description": "Tell the person's app which port on this session's machine serves a web page they may want to look at. The person may be following this session from a phone or a browser, where a preview pane can show that page. The app does not know the port unless you say so.\n\n- Call this when a dev server or other web server is up and the person may want to see its page, or when you are about to point them to one that was already running. Give the port it really listens on: read it from the server's output, not from what you asked for, since many servers move to the next free port.\n- Call it once for each server, and again only if that server's port changes. A restart on the same port needs no new call.\n- When more than one server runs, give each a short name, such as \"web\" or \"docs\".\n- Do not call it for a database, a debugger, an API with no page, a port nothing listens on, or a server you start only for your own tests.\n- This tool only states the port: it starts nothing and opens nothing. The app decides what to show and the person decides whether to open it. Not every app shows a preview, so do not tell the person one is ready because you called this.\n- If a tool whose name ends in preview_start started the server, that call already gave the port, so do not call this tool for that server.", "eager_input_streaming": true, "input_schema": {"properties": {"name": {"description": "A short name for the server, such as \"web\", when more than one runs. An app may show it to the person.", "maxLength": 40, "type": "string"}, "port": {"description": "The port the server really listens on, on this session's machine.", "maximum": 65535, "minimum": 1, "type": "integer"}}, "required": ["port"], "type": "object"}, "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__report_preview_port"}, "server": "claude-code-remote"}, {"description": "Connect THIS session to a folder the user's own computer serves through Remote Control, so this session's commands and file tools can run there. Calling this tool IS the request, and a person answers it every time (no permission mode and no stored rule does). So do NOT ask in chat first; once the task needs something that only exists on the user's computer, call list_computer_folders, then call this tool with the chosen folder's environment_id, computer_name and folder copied exactly — the request is refused if they do not match the folder's record. Only a folder under `folders` in that list can be requested; for one under `cannot_be_used`, give the user its explanation instead. The call waits for the computer. This can take a minute. The computer answers only while the Claude desktop app, or Claude Code's Remote Control, is running there. The result is final: `attached`, `refused` (the computer said no, and the result says why), or `did_not_answer` (no computer attached: it did not answer in time, or the user cancelled the request while it waited; nothing is left pending). A session is attached to at most one folder at a time, and it stays tied to the first computer it attaches to: to use a different computer, the user starts a new session. Asking again for the folder the session is attached to reconnects it: that attachment ends first, then the computer is asked afresh. While this session is attached to a folder (list_computer_folders shows it as `current`), do not call this tool for a different folder, and while an earlier request is still waiting, do not call it at all: the user would be shown the permission prompt, and the request is refused even if they allow it. If the user wants a different folder, tell them which computer and folder this session is connected to, and that they can disconnect it (how is below) and then ask you again. If the request is refused or the computer does not answer, do not re-ask in chat; carry on without it and say what remains blocked, including what the refusal says. You have no tool that disconnects the computer: only the user can. If they ask how: from this conversation's menu or its connected-computer indicator on claude.ai; or on that computer, wherever they set up Remote Control there (in the Claude desktop app: Settings, Claude Code, Remote Control; if the folder is served by the `claude remote-control` command, by stopping that command, which disconnects every session using the folder). The desktop app's settings page is also where they choose which folders are in the list.", "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__request_computer_folder", "schema": {"description": "Connect THIS session to a folder the user's own computer serves through Remote Control, so this session's commands and file tools can run there. Calling this tool IS the request, and a person answers it every time (no permission mode and no stored rule does). So do NOT ask in chat first; once the task needs something that only exists on the user's computer, call list_computer_folders, then call this tool with the chosen folder's environment_id, computer_name and folder copied exactly — the request is refused if they do not match the folder's record. Only a folder under `folders` in that list can be requested; for one under `cannot_be_used`, give the user its explanation instead. The call waits for the computer. This can take a minute. The computer answers only while the Claude desktop app, or Claude Code's Remote Control, is running there. The result is final: `attached`, `refused` (the computer said no, and the result says why), or `did_not_answer` (no computer attached: it did not answer in time, or the user cancelled the request while it waited; nothing is left pending). A session is attached to at most one folder at a time, and it stays tied to the first computer it attaches to: to use a different computer, the user starts a new session. Asking again for the folder the session is attached to reconnects it: that attachment ends first, then the computer is asked afresh. While this session is attached to a folder (list_computer_folders shows it as `current`), do not call this tool for a different folder, and while an earlier request is still waiting, do not call it at all: the user would be shown the permission prompt, and the request is refused even if they allow it. If the user wants a different folder, tell them which computer and folder this session is connected to, and that they can disconnect it (how is below) and then ask you again. If the request is refused or the computer does not answer, do not re-ask in chat; carry on without it and say what remains blocked, including what the refusal says. You have no tool that disconnects the computer: only the user can. If they ask how: from this conversation's menu or its connected-computer indicator on claude.ai; or on that computer, wherever they set up Remote Control there (in the Claude desktop app: Settings, Claude Code, Remote Control; if the folder is served by the `claude remote-control` command, by stopping that command, which disconnects every session using the folder). The desktop app's settings page is also where they choose which folders are in the list.", "eager_input_streaming": true, "input_schema": {"properties": {"computer_name": {"description": "The folder's computer_name from list_computer_folders, exactly.", "type": "string"}, "environment_id": {"description": "The folder's environment_id from list_computer_folders.", "type": "string"}, "folder": {"description": "The folder's name from list_computer_folders, exactly.", "type": "string"}}, "required": ["environment_id", "computer_name", "folder"], "type": "object"}, "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__request_computer_folder"}, "server": "claude-code-remote"}, {"description": "Ask the user for an API credential for a host. Ask as soon as you know the work needs a key for a host, before any call to the host fails: when the user or setup docs say a token or API key is needed, or when the work must make real calls to an API that no connector covers and you hold no key for, even if the network policy also blocks its host. Also ask when an HTTPS request fails with 401/403 for lack of credentials. Ask before you report no access or put made-up responses in the API's place. This is the preferred way to give a session access to an API that needs a token or API key, rather than asking the user to put one in an environment variable. Never ask the user to paste secrets into the chat; where you would tell them not to, call this instead. Calling this shows the user a secure form. A credential they save is stored on this session's environment, and the network proxy injects it into requests to that host from every session in that environment, including other people's sessions when the environment is shared: this session usually within a few seconds, and later sessions. Saving it also allows HTTPS requests to that host from those sessions, so do not add the host to the environment's allowed hosts for it. If get_environment is among your tools, first make the move its description calls for, unless the user asked for the credential here. If the credential will be saved on the user's Default all the same, because the session has no repository or could not be moved, say so in your message, then call this tool, and do not propose a move for a credential again in this session. If the user declined the move, call this tool only if they then ask for the credential. You never receive the value — the result only says whether a credential is now configured, and if not, why. If the call itself is denied, because the user declined or because the app they are using cannot show the form, no result comes back: do not call this again for that host, and tell them the credential can be added in the environment's settings, then retry the failing request once it has been. After `saved`, retry the failing request right away. Until the proxy picks the credential up, the request can still fail with 401/403 or with `request blocked: no rule allows host`: if it is refused again with one of those, wait about a minute and retry once more rather than change network settings. Do not wait for the proxy after a timeout or any other error. This cannot help with secrets that must exist inside the sandbox (database passwords, container registry pulls, SSH keys), GitHub (use the repository tools), or public package registries the proxy does not handle. A local key check is no reason to skip this tool. If a CLI or SDK will not start without a key and sends it in a header, give it a dummy value in the format it expects for that command, never a real one: the proxy replaces it.", "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__request_credential", "schema": {"description": "Ask the user for an API credential for a host. Ask as soon as you know the work needs a key for a host, before any call to the host fails: when the user or setup docs say a token or API key is needed, or when the work must ma