 401/403 or with `request blocked: no rule allows host`: if it is refused again with one of those, wait about a minute and retry once more rather than change network settings. Do not wait for the proxy after a timeout or any other error. This cannot help with secrets that must exist inside the sandbox (database passwords, container registry pulls, SSH keys), GitHub (use the repository tools), or public package registries the proxy does not handle. A local key check is no reason to skip this tool. If a CLI or SDK will not start without a key and sends it in a header, give it a dummy value in the format it expects for that command, never a real one: the proxy replaces it.", "eager_input_streaming": true, "input_schema": {"properties": {"display_name": {"description": "Optional short label to prefill in the form, e.g. \"Example API token\"; it names the credential in the environment's network secrets list. The user can change it. Plain text only: no URLs, links or markup.", "type": "string"}, "header_name": {"description": "Optional. The HTTP header the API reads the key from, e.g. \"xi-api-key\" or \"X-Api-Key\". Send it only when the user or the API's documentation says the key goes in a header other than Authorization: Bearer; leave it out otherwise. One word of letters, digits, hyphens and underscores: at most 40 characters, in at most 5 hyphen-separated parts of at most 16 characters each, and the last part must be key, apikey, token, auth, authorization, secret, credential or credentials. The form cannot save a key under any other header: for an API that reads one, the user adds the key in the environment's settings. Prefills the form; the user can change it. Not used with suggested_type \"basic\". Never put a key's value in this or any other field.", "type": "string"}, "host": {"description": "The exact hostname the credential is for, e.g. \"api.example.com\". Lowercase DNS name only — no scheme, path, port, IP address or wildcard. Shown to the user in the form.